//! A typed gRPC client of a build node's tierstore trees interface
//! (`tierstore.trees.v1.TreesService`, generated by tierstore's `tierstore-trees-api` crate at
//! the one tierstore revision the workspace pins), for the frontend's executor
//! (`docs/design/trees-for-action-inputs.md`, section 8).
//!
//! The interface creates a tree on the node, its files spans of objects the node reads into
//! its cache, and places it where a job's mount finds it. This crate wraps its three calls and
//! nothing else: [`Client::create_tree`], [`Client::get_placement`] and
//! [`Client::delete_tree`]. It takes and returns the generated messages ([`v1`]), so there is
//! no second copy of them to keep in step, and checks what the caller keeps of an answer: the
//! node's digest of the tree ([`TreeDigest`], 32 bytes) and the placement ([`PlacementId`]).
//! kbf computes no tree digest of its own; a request names a child tree only by the digest a
//! node returned, so a node of any revision of tierstore's digest builder works with this
//! client.
//!
//! One client calls one node ([`Node`], `host:port`, as a host's address label carries it).
//! No code of the farm calls it yet: the executor's stage that creates each job's input tree
//! comes after it.
//!
//! # Signatures, and the key
//!
//! Each call is signed as tierstore asks (its `server/trees/mod.rs`): AWS Signature Version 4
//! with one of the node's access keys ([`Key`]), over `POST`, the gRPC path, the call's
//! authority as `host`, `x-amz-date`, and `x-amz-content-sha256`, the hex SHA-256 of the whole
//! request body, which the node checks against the body it reads. The secret signs and is
//! never sent; the key's `Debug` prints its id alone. The signing reads a [`Clock`] (the
//! system's unless the configuration names another), since the node refuses a call more than
//! 15 minutes from its own time.
//!
//! # The connection
//!
//! With a pinned CA ([`Config::with_ca`]) the client calls over TLS 1.2 or 1.3 with the `ring`
//! provider, trusting that CA and no system root, and checks that the node's certificate
//! carries its host; it presents no client certificate. Without one, it calls in clear, which
//! it does for a loopback node, and for a node off loopback only with
//! [`Config::allow_plaintext`]: a signed call carries no secret, but its tree and sources are
//! readable on the network and the call can be replayed for 15 minutes.
//!
//! # Errors
//!
//! [`Error`] separates what the caller does next. [`Error::Unreachable`] means nothing was
//! sent. [`Error::Transport`] and [`Error::Decode`] mean the node may have acted
//! ([`Error::may_have_applied`]); every call is idempotent, so the caller repeats it.
//! [`Error::Refused`] is a refusal of the node by name ([`Reason`], with what it is about);
//! [`Error::Status`] is any other answer that says the node did not act. The client never
//! retries on its own.
//!
//! # The fake
//!
//! With the `fake` feature, [`fake::FakeTreesServer`] is a trees interface on loopback that
//! checks each call's signature as the node does and keeps the node's rules: an idempotent
//! create by the tree and its locations, a location taken by another tree, `unknown child
//! tree`, a source the test marks missing, short or lying, a cache too small for a tree
//! under `wait`, a client digest that is not the node's, `unknown placement`, and a digest of
//! its own that is not one kbf could compute. It exists so the code above this crate is
//! tested against the node's contract and not against a mock that agrees with it.
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

mod client;
mod codec;
mod config;
mod dial;
mod error;
#[cfg(feature = "fake")]
pub mod fake;
mod sign;
mod signing;
mod types;

